# Line endings: NO CONVERSION, ANYWHERE.
#
# Added 2026-08-02 with the move of the weekly run from a Linux GitHub runner to
# the Windows company server.
#
# What is actually in this repository, measured with `git show HEAD:<file>`:
# the blobs are MIXED. canada_trq_tracker.py, README.md, download_latest.bat and
# the test fixtures are stored CRLF; docs/known-issues.md, enduser/guide.html and
# the workflow YAML are stored LF. Nothing enforced either, so it drifted with
# whatever tool last wrote each file.
#
# That mix is exactly why this matters. Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, which converts CRLF->LF on `git add`. On the server that
# would silently renormalise every CRLF-stored file the moment anything touched
# its mtime, turning them all into whole-file diffs and leaving the working tree
# permanently "modified" — and the weekly task refuses to publish when the tree
# is dirty outside data/. The job would stop, every week, for a reason that has
# nothing to do with the data.
#
# `* -text` pins the bytes: each blob is checked out verbatim and staged
# verbatim, on every OS, regardless of anyone's core.autocrlf. Because it
# preserves whatever each file already had rather than imposing one style, it
# produces ZERO diff — which matters when it lands the day before a cutover.
# (The server clone additionally sets core.autocrlf=false — belt and braces,
# since config does not survive a re-clone by someone who forgets.)
#
# Consequence for editors: whatever you use must preserve a file's existing
# endings. A tool that rewrites LF as CRLF turns a two-line change into a
# whole-file diff. Check with `git diff --stat` before committing; a paragraph
# edit that reports hundreds of changed lines is this, not your edit.
* -text

# The workbook is the accumulated history and a zip container. `binary` adds
# -diff on top of -text, so nothing ever attempts a textual diff or merge of it.
# The weekly task deliberately fails rather than auto-resolving a conflict here:
# picking a side blindly would silently discard a week of history.
*.xlsx binary
